@startuml component_diagram

' =====================================================================
'  COMPONENT VIEW — the 30-second read of the architecture.
'
'  Derived from _model.iuml, which stays the reference: this diagram
'  adds nothing, it only drops everything that is not a package
'  boundary or a seam between two packages.
'
'  What it is FOR: showing that the dependency graph is a DAG, and that
'  the four abstractions (Coordinator, Dispatcher, SimulationDriver,
'  StepSnapshot) are what keep it one. Every arrow points DOWN, towards
'  something more abstract or more foundational — that is the whole
'  design in one picture.
'
'  What it deliberately does NOT show: classes, methods, multiplicities,
'  nor report/ — those live in the class diagram and in the report
'  diagram; repeating them here would make this a second, competing
'  rendering of the same thing.
'
'  LAYOUT: every edge carries an explicit direction and the components
'  are declared top-down in reading order. Without that, dot lays the
'  graph out by edge cost alone and the picture ends up contradicting
'  the legend that tells you to read it downward.
' =====================================================================

title Component diagram — packages, and the seams between them

skinparam componentStyle uml2
skinparam ArrowColor #333333
skinparam ComponentBorderColor #4A90D9
skinparam InterfaceBorderColor #C9722A
skinparam InterfaceBackgroundColor #FFF5EA
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7
skinparam NoteBorderColor #C9B458

' ── Layer 1 — presentation, and the one observer ──────────────────
component "**client · frontends**\npygame · headless" as frontends #FFF5EA

' report/ is drawn small and grey on purpose: its internals belong to
' report_diagram.puml, not here. It appears at all because it is the
' SECOND implementer of SimulationDriver — and an interface with a
' single visible client always reads as gratuitous indirection.
component "**report**" as report #F0F0F0

' ── Layer 2 — the outward-facing seam ─────────────────────────────
interface "SimulationDriver" as IDriver
interface "StepSnapshot" as ISnap

' ── Layer 3 — the composition root ────────────────────────────────
component "**client · control**\nscripting API\ncomposition root" as control #FFF5EA

' ── Layer 4 — the interchangeable parts ───────────────────────────
component "**algo**\nhow they get there" as algo #F0FFF0
component "**mrta**\nwho does what" as mrta #FFF0F5
component "**client · view**\nfrozen frames" as view #FFF5EA

' ── Layer 5 — the engine seams, declared by core ──────────────────
interface "Coordinator" as ICoord
interface "Dispatcher" as IDisp

' ── Layer 6 — the foundation ──────────────────────────────────────
component "**core**\nworld · grid · tick" as core #EAF4FF

' =====================================================================
'  Relations. Solid = uses the types directly. Dashed = plugs into an
'  abstraction it does not own.
' =====================================================================

' Presentation drives the engine through the interface only.
frontends ..down.> IDriver : drives
frontends ..down.> ISnap   : reads

' The same interface, implemented rather than consumed: RunCollector
' wraps a driver and passes for one. This is what pays for the
' abstraction — a decorator must present the face of what it wraps.
' It reads the same frames a display does: measuring the run needs no
' more access than drawing it, which is why no arrow from report reaches
' past these two interfaces.
report ..down.> IDriver : "implements\n(decorates)"
report ..down.> ISnap   : measures

' control declares that seam and implements it.
IDriver -down- control
ISnap   -down- control

' The composition root is the only component naming a concrete class.
control -down-> algo : "picks the\ncoordinator"
control -down-> mrta : wires the fleet
control -down-> view : builds snapshots

' The two implementations plug into the seams core declares.
algo ..down.> ICoord : implements
mrta ..down.> IDisp  : implements

' Plain dependencies, no inversion: these simply use the types below.
' Labels are VERBS, never type names. An arrow here says who depends on
' whom; a bare "TaskState" sitting on the line reads as cargo instead,
' and the reader infers the supplier is at the tail — the exact
' opposite of what the arrow means.
algo -right-> mrta : "uses Allocator, Task"
view -left-> mrta  : uses TaskState
algo -down-> core  : uses
mrta -down-> core  : uses
view -down-> core  : uses Position

' core owns the two engine seams: it declares them and calls them,
' without ever knowing who implements them.
ICoord -down- core
IDisp  -down- core

' The long edge on the side is real: build() assembles the Simulation
' itself. It is drawn because "the composition root touches core" is
' exactly the point of a composition root.
control -down-> core : builds Simulation

' No notes: this diagram is the 30-second read, and the rationale it
' would carry is already in the class diagram and in AGENT.md.

legend bottom left
  **How to read it**
  Every arrow points downward: the graph is a DAG, and **core** — at
  the bottom — depends on nothing.
    solid   A uses B's types directly
    dashed  A plugs into an abstraction it does not own
  The interfaces are where the graph would otherwise cycle. Without
  them core would have to import algo, and the display would have to
  import the engine.
end legend

@enduml
